# v3 engine limits: MAX_CHANNELS 11 -> 12/13 behind cargo features, and a
# versioned wide certificate container (v2) for proofs that outgrow the u32
# step-id space.
#
# Applies on top of, in order:
#   tools/patches/sortnetopt-macos-proc.patch
#   tools/patches/sortnetopt-instrumentation-v3.patch
#   tools/patches/sortnetopt-online-subsumption-v3.patch
#   tools/patches/sortnetopt-perf-v3.patch
#   tools/patches/sortnetopt-tier1-v3.patch
#   tools/patches/sortnetopt-tier2a-v3.patch
#   tools/patches/sortnetopt-tier2b-v3.patch
# against the pinned upstream commit 0b5d09c47446096f9e3a0812b35afc72b7f2a718.
# Apply with:  patch -V none -p1 -i <this file>
#
# checker/ is NOT touched. Six files change: Cargo.toml, src/output_set.rs,
# src/output_set/packed.rs, src/search/states.rs, src/search.rs (one
# visibility keyword), src/proof.rs, src/bin/sortnetopt.rs.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DEFAULT BUILD IS UNCHANGED, BY CONSTRUCTION AND BY MEASUREMENT
#
# `MAX_CHANNELS` is selected by cargo feature: none = 11 (upstream), `wide12`
# = 12, `wide13` = 13. With no feature every constant, every array capacity,
# every match arm and every emitted byte is what it was before this patch, so
# the n<=11 regression bar is met structurally rather than argued.
#
# This is a feature and not a plain constant raise because several hot-path
# types are fixed-capacity arrays sized from MAX_CHANNELS. `PackedSet` is
# `{usize, usize, [u64; 2^MAX_CHANNELS/64]}` and is `Copy`; `Subsume` holds two
# by value, so every exact subsumption test copies 2 x 272 bytes at
# MAX_CHANNELS=11 and 2 x 1040 at 13. `BVec<u16>`, the BFS queue inside
# `prune_extremal_channel_into`, is a 4 KB stack object at 11 and 16 KB at 13.
# `PVec<u8>`, rebuilt on every `StateMap` get and set, goes 256 -> 1024 bytes.
# None of this is stored - every persistent structure (the memo table's
# `BTreeMap` keys, the subsumption index's flat `Vec<u8>` payload arrays) is
# already sized from the actual channel count, which is why the raise costs
# only transient/stack space. But it is enough to be measurable, so it is
# gated.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHAT HAD TO CHANGE, AND WHY EACH ONE WOULD HAVE BROKEN SILENTLY
#
#  1. `AVec` capacity (src/output_set.rs). Upstream hard-codes
#     `ArrayVec<[T; 512]>` while the value it must hold is
#     `MAX_ABSTRACTION_SIZE = MAX_CHANNELS * (MAX_CHANNELS - 1) * 4` - 440 at
#     n=11 (fits), 528 at n=12 and 624 at n=13 (do not). A bare constant bump
#     overflows the ArrayVec inside `OutputSet::abstraction`. Capacity is now
#     512 for the default build and 768 (the next size `arrayvec` 0.5
#     implements) for the wide builds, with a compile-time assertion that it
#     is at least `MAX_ABSTRACTION_SIZE`.
#
#  2. `histogram_signature` (src/output_set/packed.rs) - THE ONE REAL BUG.
#     The Tier-1 thermometer pre-filter packs 5 bits per weight class and
#     there are `channels + 1` classes, so it needs `(channels+1)*5 <= 64`.
#     That holds exactly up to channels == 11 (60 bits) and FAILS at 12 (65).
#     `1u64 << 65` is a shift-out-of-range: in a release build the shift
#     amount wraps modulo 64 and the signature is silently scrambled. It is
#     not unsound in the "accepts a bad proof" direction - the filter is only
#     a necessary condition, so corruption can only make it reject candidates
#     that really do subsume - but a missed subsumer strands witnesses at
#     proof time and `GenProof::prove_all` panics with "no valid proof step
#     found" after the whole search has already been paid for. The slot width
#     is now `hist_sig_bits(channels) = min(5, 64/(channels+1))`, which is 5
#     for every channels <= 11 (so the default build's signatures are
#     bit-identical) and 4 at 12 and 13. The threshold denominator follows the
#     slot width, so the thermometer stays monotone and the filter stays
#     one-sided at every width. New test
#     `histogram_signature_fits_and_is_monotone_at_every_width` asserts both
#     properties for every width up to MAX_CHANNELS.
#
#  3. `OutputSetMap` (src/search/states.rs). Upstream has hand-written arms
#     for widths 3..11 only, and the fall-through is asymmetric: `set_` and
#     `remove_with_packed` hit `unreachable!()`, but `get_with_packed` returns
#     `None`. So a missing arm does not crash on the read path - it reports
#     "not memoised" for every query at that width, turning the DP into
#     unbounded re-exploration, visible only as inexplicable slowness. Arms
#     for 12 and 13 are added under the features, using flat `[u8; N]` keys
#     (the 9/10/11 arms use nested `[[u8; 32]; m]` only because in 2020 arrays
#     implemented `Ord` only up to length 32; the two layouts have identical
#     bytes and identical lexicographic order, so the `BTreeMap` behaviour is
#     the same). The `unreachable!()`s now name the feature to rebuild with.
#     New test `output_set_map_has_an_arm_at_every_width` exercises
#     set/get/for_each/census/remove at every width 3..=MAX_CHANNELS; nothing
#     else in the suite touches a width above 6.
#
#  4. `known_bounds` seed table (src/search/states.rs), extended from 11 to 13
#     entries: S(12) = 39 (proved, arXiv:2012.04400) and 45 for width 13, the
#     size of the best known 13-channel network. These seed `bounds[1]` only;
#     the lower seed stays at the universal 1. Indices 0..=11 are untouched,
#     so n<=11 searches seed identically. Without them a width-12/13 node
#     seeds at the quadratic fallback, 66 and 78.
#
#  5. `cmd_search` had NO width check - only a `debug_assert!` inside
#     `OutputSet::all_values`, which release builds drop. An over-wide request
#     failed deep inside an ArrayVec capacity panic. It now fails immediately
#     and names the feature to rebuild with.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CERTIFICATE CONTAINER v2 (src/proof.rs; spec in docs/certificate-format-v2.md)
#
# The legacy container writes the step count and every witness step id as a
# `u32`, capping a proof at 4.29e9 steps against an n=13 estimate of 1.5e11.
# The frozen Isabelle-extracted checker reads that format and must not change,
# so this patch is emitter-side only and strictly additive:
#
#   * `write_proof_v1` is upstream's `write_proof`, byte for byte. It is taken
#     whenever the step count fits in a u32, which is every proof the
#     programme can currently produce (n=11: 1.3e7 steps, a factor of ~340
#     below the cap). Verified: running `gen-proof` on ONE fixed pruned
#     directory with the pre-patch binary, this binary, and the wide13 binary
#     produced byte-identical `proof.bin` at n=9 and n=10.
#
#   * `write_proof_v2` emits the wide container: 8-byte magic "SNOCERT2",
#     version, flags, u64 step count / offsets / lengths, an FNV-1a-64 header
#     hash, a SHA-256 over the step table plus payloads, and an end magic.
#     Step payloads are the same bytes as v1 except that witness step ids are
#     8 bytes rather than 4.
#
#   * The choice is made BEFORE encoding, from `output_sets.len()` (an upper
#     bound on the step count), because the id width is baked into the
#     payloads and so cannot be decided from the final count.
#     `SORTNETOPT_CERT_FORMAT=v2` forces it, for producing small v2 files to
#     test against; `=v1` forces the legacy container and asserts rather than
#     truncating.
#
#   * The SHA-256 is the dependency-free one Tier-2b already ships in
#     `search/checkpoint.rs` (unit-tested against the FIPS 180-4 vectors);
#     the only change to that module is `mod` -> `pub mod`. NO NEW CRATES.
#
# The verified checker cannot read v2 and is not asked to. `tools/cert_v2.py`
# is an unverified reference reader/checker/transcoder; extending the Isabelle
# checker is a separate campaign.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
diff -ruN a/Cargo.toml b/Cargo.toml
--- a/Cargo.toml	2026-08-17 22:46:42
+++ b/Cargo.toml	2026-08-17 22:53:49
@@ -12,6 +12,27 @@
 [profile.bench]
